            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus identifies 'coin' as a highly context-dependent symbol with no single meaning. Depending on the passage, it can symbolize earthly wealth and mammon, secular authority and obligation (tribute money), sacrificial giving (widow's mites), the price of betrayal (thirty pieces of silver), or a lost sinner/soul bearing God's image (the lost coin, and patristic readings of the tribute money).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book synthesizes the symbolism into a single, unified concept: a person as an owner's stamped image-bearer. This encompasses bearing the name or image of God (or the beast), being assigned a value, and functioning as a repentant sinner who is sought and recovered.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Multiple disparate meanings including earthly wealth/stewardship, civic authority, and a human soul bearing God's image.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A person as the owner's stamped image-bearer and a repentant sinner.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus treats 'coin' as having multiple distinct literal and symbolic applications based on the narrative (including wealth, betrayal, and stewardship). The book narrows the definition to the specific theological concept of the person as an image-bearer/sinner, excluding coins as mere material wealth, while extending the coinage metaphor (stamping an image/name) to the marks received in Revelation.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"There is no material contradiction in the core referent. The book adopts and elevates one prominent strand of the consensus (the deeply rooted patristic reading of the lost coin and the tribute money) as the universal definition for the symbol.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book takes the recognized 'image-bearer' and 'lost sinner' consensus alternatives and makes them the central definition. This refines the consensus by synthesizing a unified symbolic referent ('a person') rather than treating 'coin' as a fractured, context-dependent symbol. The inclusion of Revelation passages further shows a reclassification by treating 'marks on foreheads' conceptually as coinage.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"By unifying the symbol of the coin around the concept of a person as an image-bearer, the book struggles to comfortably account for passages where coins function primarily as symbols of earthly stewardship, greed, or betrayal (such as Judas's thirty pieces of silver or teachings on mammon).\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
